  "worked_solution": "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/ed8a998b543cb951a12599de55004c9220a53ac252a9eea66577475e2b8b6093.png)\n\nThe upper sum $U(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={M}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{M}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{M}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{M}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=1$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $U(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+{M}_{12}+{M}_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}-2$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}-2$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/a0033237f985416771125780a3d9b68bdfd8c6c33b9a4b0f2a32fa7af4c59bca.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/26cae8b6e2c69c2c0c66d78eb64c5d02fc500d10a970e54b09f6ee72bc145b74.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{11} & =f(4,2)=26 \\\\ {M}_{21} & =f(5,2)=35 \\\\ {M}_{12} & =f(4,3)=41 \\\\ {M}_{22} & =f(5,3)=50\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =26+35+41+50 \\\\  & =152.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
